Fuzz targets for Qt Quick 3D, to be built and run with clang's libFuzzer.

The layout and the build recipe follow qtbase's tests/libfuzzer; see the README
there for the full instructions. As in qtbase there is no top level
CMakeLists.txt: each target is a standalone project, so none of this enters the
normal build and none of it costs anything in CI.

In short: configure a Qt with

    configure -sanitize address -sanitize undefined -sanitize fuzzer-no-link

build qtquick3d against it, then build a target on its own with

    <sanitized qt>/bin/qt-cmake -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
        -S utils/qssgmesh/read -B /tmp/meshfuzz \
        && cmake --build /tmp/meshfuzz

and run it over a seed corpus:

    /tmp/meshfuzz/read <corpus dir> \
        -dict=utils/qssgmesh/read/read.dict \
        -rss_limit_mb=2048 -malloc_limit_mb=512 -timeout=25

The seed corpora are whatever is to hand and are deliberately not duplicated
here; what to start from is listed with each target below. Minimise any finding
with -minimize_crash=1 and commit it to the fuzz data directory of the matching
autotest, where it is picked up automatically as a regression test.

utils/qssgmesh/read
    The whole .mesh path: the container footer and entry list, then the mesh
    header, the buffers, the subsets and their names, and the morph target
    reconstruction that older versions of the format need. Every count and
    offset in the file is attacker controlled and most of them size an
    allocation or bound a loop. Seeds: the .mesh files under tests/ and
    examples/, which cover every supported version of the format. Findings go
    in tests/auto/utils/mesh/data/fuzz.

texturehelpers/qssgexrreader/read
    The whole file path: magic, version, attributes, offset table and every
    chunk. Seeds: the fixture set in tests/auto/exr/data. Findings go in
    tests/auto/exr/data/fuzz.

texturehelpers/qssgexrcodecs/piz
    The PIZ Huffman and wavelet stages on their own, which the file level
    target rarely reaches past header validation.
